/**
@mainpage Tower Defense (COMP 345 - Assignment 3)

@section team Team
Jana Alnoman, Chloe Fokom, Emine Nisa Mahmutogullari, Adila Burga (COMP 345).

@section overview Overview
A tower defense game in C++. Critters enter the map at one point and walk a
path to the exit. The player spends coins on towers placed on the scenery
around the path to kill the critters before they escape. Assignment 3 adds
three design patterns, all used by the playable game: Strategy (how towers
choose a target), Factory (the critters of each wave) and Decorator (tower
upgrades and effects). The Observer pattern of Assignment 2 is also kept.

@section rules Game rules

@subsection rules_map Map
- The map is a grid of cells: scenery (towers can be placed), path, one entry
  and one exit. A map is valid only if the path links the entry to the exit
  (Map::isValid()).

@subsection rules_towers Towers
- Towers are bought with coins and placed on scenery cells only.
- DirectDamageTower hits one critter, AreaDamageTower hits every critter in
  range, SlowTower hits one critter and halves its speed.
- A tower can be leveled up to level 3. The next level costs the buying cost
  times the current level; each level adds 5 power and 1.0 range, and the
  sell refund grows by half of the price paid (LevelUpDecorator).
- A tower can also get the effects Splash, Burning and Freezing, one of each,
  for a price (SplashDecorator, BurningDecorator, FreezingDecorator).
- Each tower has a targeting strategy that decides which critter in range it
  hits (TargetingStrategy). The default is nearest to the exit.

@subsection rules_critters Critters and waves
- A critter has a level, hit points, strength, speed, reward and a kind
  (Critter).
- A killed critter gives its reward in coins. A critter that reaches the exit
  costs one life and coins equal to its strength.
- Burning (Critter::ignite()) deals damage every second for a few seconds.
  Freezing (Critter::freeze()) halves the speed for a few seconds.
- Wave n has 3 + n critters. Which kinds appear depends on the number of waves
  already destroyed (WaveFactory).

@subsection rules_player Player
- The player starts with 350 coins and 10 lives and wins by destroying 10
  waves. Coins are spent on towers, level ups and effects, earned by kills,
  and lost to critters that reach the exit. The balance never goes below 0.

@section strategy Strategy pattern: choosing a target
TargetingStrategy is the abstract strategy. A Tower owns one strategy object
and calls Tower::chooseTarget() with the critters in range (as a list of
TargetCandidate, which includes the distance to the tower). Four concrete
strategies decide which one to hit:
| Strategy | Chooses |
|----------|---------|
| NearestToTowerStrategy | the smallest distance to the tower |
| NearestToExitStrategy | the critter that is furthest along the path |
| StrongestStrategy | the most hit points |
| WeakestStrategy | the fewest hit points |

The strategy can be replaced at any time with Tower::setStrategy() (the player
presses T in the game). Tower does not need to know how the choice is made, and
a new strategy only needs a new subclass.

@section factory Factory pattern: creating waves
CritterFactory is the abstract factory with the method
CritterFactory::createCritter(). It has four subclasses, each creating one
kind of critter with its own characteristics:
NormalCritterFactory, FastCritterFactory, TankCritterFactory and
BossCritterFactory. WaveFactory owns them and, in WaveFactory::createWave(),
chooses a factory for each critter of the wave from the number of waves
already destroyed:
- Normal critters always;
- Fast critters from wave 3 (every third critter);
- Tank critters from wave 5 (every fourth critter);
- a Boss at the end of the waves 8 and later.

The critters also get stronger with the wave number. Game only calls
WaveFactory::createWave(); it does not know the kinds.

@section decorator Decorator pattern: upgrading towers
TowerDecorator is a Tower that wraps another Tower (the "component") and
forwards every virtual call to it. A subclass changes only what it needs, so
decorators can be stacked in any order (for example a level up wrapped in
Burning wrapped in Splash).
| Decorator | Effect |
|-----------|--------|
| LevelUpDecorator | level +1, power +5, range +1, higher refund; adds the extra power to each attack |
| SplashDecorator | critters near the target take half the damage; refund +half of the price |
| BurningDecorator | the target burns (a quarter of the power each second for 3 s) |
| FreezingDecorator | the target moves at half speed for 2 s |
EffectDecorator is the common base of the two effects that act on the hit
critters (burning and freezing). Because every getter of Tower is virtual, the
cost of the next level (Tower::getUpgradeCost()), the sell refund, the range
and the power always come from the outermost decorator. Game keeps each tower
as a std::unique_ptr<Tower> and replaces it by the decorated tower when the
player upgrades it.

@section observer Observer design pattern (Assignment 2)
Map, Tower, Critter and CritterGroup inherit from Observable. Views inherit
from Observer and register with Observable::attach(); when the subject changes
it calls notifyObservers(), which marks the view as out of date so the SFML
window redraws itself on its next frame. The four views are MapObserver,
TowerObserver, CritterObserver and CritterGroupObserver (demonstration in
main.cpp).

@section playable The playable game
Game holds the rules (placing, leveling up, adding effects, selling towers,
waves, shooting, coins and lives) and has no graphics. game_main.cpp draws it in
one SFML window and turns mouse clicks and key presses into calls on Game.
The controls are listed in the README and in game_main.cpp.

@section tests Test cases
Tests_A3.cpp is a console program with checks for the strategies, the
factories, the decorators (alone and stacked) and the Game that uses them.

@section scope Scope of this build
Not implemented: a map editor, a choice between several maps (the game uses
one built-in map) and saving or loading maps.

@section build Building and generating this documentation
See README.md for build and run instructions. To regenerate this
documentation, run `doxygen Doxyfile` in this folder and open
`docs/html/index.html`.
*/
